## Notes for the command author

(Delete this section before committing.)

A good slash command:
- Is for a workflow you do **at least once a day** (otherwise the upfront design cost doesn't pay back)
- Pre-computes context via inline bash (saves 3-5 tool-call round-trips)
- Has hard NOs that match the project's golden rules
- Names each step explicitly so the user can interrupt at any point
- Never destructively modifies state without explicit per-call confirmation
- Never bypasses safety hooks (it doesn't override `Stop` hooks etc.)

A bad slash command:
- Wraps a one-step workflow (just type the bash command directly)
- Reads contextual state inside the prompt body (slow — use pre-flight bash)
- Auto-confirms destructive operations
- Re-implements features Claude Code already has
- Has no hard NOs and trusts the user to remember the golden rules

### Worked example: `/commit-push-pr`

The reference implementation is the `/commit-push-pr` workflow from Boris Cherny's setup, codified for any project's golden rules:

```yaml
---
description: Validate → commit → push → open/update PR against <BASE_BRANCH>. Enforces golden rules §1 (no <PROD_BRANCH> pushes) and §2 (build before commit).
allowed-tools: Bash, Read, Edit
argument-hint: [optional commit summary override]
---
```

Pre-flight context: current branch, working tree status, diff stat against `<BASE_BRANCH>`, build-relevant files dirty list, existing PR JSON, recent commits, `<BASE_BRANCH>`'s recent commit style. (~7 inline bash invocations.)

Workflow:
1. Branch safety (refuse `<PROD_BRANCH>` and `<BASE_BRANCH>` — work happens on a feature branch)
2. Build gate (skip if no build-relevant files dirty; else run build)
3. Diff review (cross-check that PreToolUse rule-surfacing already ran)
4. Selective staging (NEVER `git add -A`; deny `.env*`, secrets)
5. Conventional Commit message with project trailer
6. Push without `--force` / `--no-verify`
7. Open or update PR against `<BASE_BRANCH>` via `gh pr create`
8. Compact final report

Hard NOs: never push to `<PROD_BRANCH>` or `<BASE_BRANCH>` directly, never force-push, never amend (always create new commit), never stage secrets, never auto-merge.

